# The `img-src`/`media-src` https: allowance is for plugin panels, not for the
# app's own chrome — every image ADE ships is same-origin or a data: URI.
#
# A plugin panel's `image` and `video` nodes carry a `src` the plugin authored,
# and the renderer already decides which of those it will load: `MEDIA_SCHEMES`
# in `components/plugins/vocabularyComponents.tsx` admits exactly `https:` and
# `data:` and refuses everything else, with the identical rule enforced on iOS
# in `PluginVocabularyMediaViews.swift`. Without the allowance here, the ONE
# client whose platform has a second opinion is the browser: the node passes the
# renderer's check, the fetch is then blocked by policy, and the reader gets a
# broken image on web and a working one everywhere else, with the reason visible
# only in a console. Naming the schemes here makes the policy match the decision
# the renderer already made rather than quietly overriding it for one client.
#
# `media-src` is spelled out rather than left to `default-src`: video and audio
# do not fall back to `img-src`, so a `video` node was refused even where the
# equivalent `image` node loaded.
# `frame-src 'self'` and `worker-src 'self'` are the plugin page tier.
#
# A plugin's `webview` surface draws in an iframe at a same-origin path that a
# service worker answers, and that response — not this policy — is what makes
# the guest's origin opaque: it carries `Content-Security-Policy: sandbox
# allow-scripts`, so the frame ADE opens here is the app's own origin while the
# document inside it has none. `frame-src` therefore names `'self'` and nothing
# else: no third-party frame, and no `blob:`/`data:` frame either, both of which
# would inherit THIS policy rather than take the worker's.
#
# `worker-src 'self'` covers registering that worker from its own bundled path.
# `blob:` was already here for the app's own workers and stays.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; media-src 'self' data: blob: https:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' wss: https:; worker-src 'self' blob:; frame-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/assets/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The plugin page space, for the one request that should never reach the origin.
#
# Every URL under here is answered by the plugin page service worker out of
# Cache Storage; the network only sees one if the worker is not controlling the
# path — an install that has not activated, or storage the browser cleared. The
# origin would then apply the single-page fallback and hand a plugin's frame the
# whole ADE client, signed in, inside an iframe.
#
# `sandbox` with no `allow-scripts` is the answer: whatever the origin serves
# here loads with an opaque origin and cannot run a line of script, so the worst
# case is an empty frame and the host's own "didn't load" card. The client also
# refuses to create the frame until the worker is active; this is the brace to
# that belt, and it is the half that survives a change to the client.
/assets/plugin-pages/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Content-Security-Policy: sandbox; default-src 'none'
  Cache-Control: no-store
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
